<article class="env-lemma" id="0CDY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CDY">Lemma <span data-tag="0CDY">33.30.2</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a faithfully flat ring map. Let $X$ be a scheme over $A$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is quasi-compact and quasi-separated, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R = H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ is a semi-local ring. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then the pullback map $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (X) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (X_ B)$ is injective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module whose pullback $\mathcal{L}'$ to $X_ B$ is trivial. Set $M = H^0(X, \mathcal{L})$ and $N = H^0(X, \mathcal{L}^{\otimes - 1})$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0CDX" data-tag="0CDX">33.30.1</a> the $R$-modules $M$ and $N$ are invertible. Since $R$ is semi-local $M \cong R$ and $N \cong R$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/02M9" data-tag="02M9">10.78.7</a>. Choose generators $s \in M$ and $t \in N$. Then $st \in R = H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ is a unit by the last part of Lemma <a href="/tag/0CDX" data-tag="0CDX">33.30.1</a>. We conclude that $s$ and $t$ define trivializations of $\mathcal{L}$ and $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes -1}$ over $X$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>